00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.840
Welcome to the AvNav video on settings and layouts. 

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:16.480
First, let’s clarify how AvNav distinguishes between these two categories.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:24.360
Settings cover all the options that  allow you, as a user, to customise  

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:30.280
the interface or, so to speak, the appearance of  AvNav. This starts with the size of the buttons  

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:36.800
and the font size on buttons or in other parts of  the programme. It continues with the colours of  

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:42.760
routes or tracks, spacing, how far the course  vector extends ahead, and so on and so forth. 

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:49.969
So there is a vast array of customisation options.  Fortunately, the new version of AvNav offers  

00:00:49.969 --> 00:00:59.440
some simplifications. But more on that later. But what, then, are layouts? Layouts summarise how  

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:06.560
You can arrange the widgets on the navigation page,  the route page and in the dashboard. Widgets are  

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:15.019
predefined areas that can display a wide variety  of content and data alongside the actual map. You  

00:01:15.019 --> 00:01:20.000
may be familiar with widgets from your mobile  phone, for example for displaying weather data. 

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.200
In the layout, you can specify which widgets  are displayed where and how they are configured. 

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:35.000
That is the key difference between settings  and layouts. Layouts cover completely  

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.240
customisable designs for the visibility of  data in AvNav. Settings control the basic  

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:47.900
interface settings and also include layouts. Let’s now start with the layouts. To do this,  

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:52.640
we’ll switch to the Layout page. As you can see, it’s structured  

00:01:53.200 --> 00:02:00.400
quite differently from the Settings page. Only the current layout can be edited at any one  

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:05.560
time. This is clearly displayed in the  first line. At the moment, the layout  

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:12.040
‘user.test’ is active. The list below  contains all the other saved layouts. 

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:18.720
The prefix ‘user’ makes it clear that this is a  layout created by the user. Layouts prefixed with  

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:40.000
‘System’ are preset and cannot be edited. You can now start editing the active  

00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:08.489
layout using the Edit button. A brief dialogue  box prompts you to enter a new name; otherwise,  

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:08.648
clicking Edit will edit the current layout. The NavPage now appears slightly reduced in  

00:03:08.648 --> 00:03:09.083
the button bar on the right has also changed. If you click directly on a widget, you can  

00:03:09.083 --> 00:03:09.720
make changes. The most important option  is likely to be the “New Widget” line,  

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:16.960
as this is where you can select an alternative  data display. Clicking on the current name  

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:26.826
opens a drop-down list of all available widgets.  We’ll select COG as an example. If you now click  

00:03:26.826 --> 00:03:29.560
directly on “Update”, you’ll change the current  widget you selected earlier. Important: Use the  

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:37.800
Before and After buttons to add a new  COG widget before or after the current  

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:45.580
one. The active one will then remain unchanged. If you want to add a new widget from the start,  

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:50.320
click in the empty area next to the  map and you will be guided through  

00:03:50.320 --> 00:04:07.080
the process of setting up a new widget. Further configuration options  

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:28.000
for the edited widget are: Caption, which is the text displayed  

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.317
alongside the value shown in the widget. Formatter. This is where the display of  

00:04:30.317 --> 00:04:30.476
the value is defined, along with the Unit  field. The format is usually already set. 

00:04:30.476 --> 00:04:31.360
Let’s take a closer look at this. We exit the  widget editing dialogue by clicking Cancel  

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:35.080
and then click the Config button. Here you can specify two things: firstly, which  

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:52.480
areas around the map should display  widgets at all, for example at the top  

00:04:52.480 --> 00:05:00.800
or bottom right. You can also specify under  which conditions the areas should display  

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:01.440
widgets, e.g. only whilst anchor watchis active.  Please refer to the documentation for further  

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:03.670
details. There are therefore very extensive  

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:07.600
options for customising the layout of the widgets. And widgets aren’t just found on the navigation  

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:28.760
page. It’s no coincidence that the  button bar includes a button for the  

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:31.665
route editor. If you click this button,  you can customise the widgets that are to  

00:05:31.665 --> 00:05:31.832
be displayed specifically on the route page. But be careful. Don’t delete the route widget,  

00:05:31.832 --> 00:05:42.540
be displayed specifically on the route page. But be careful. Don’t delete the route widget,  

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:43.316
These are also edited via layouts. To do this,  go to the dashboards page via the main menu. 

